  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1214 AM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances return to western and central NM \nSunday afternoon as the leading edge of the next major monsoonal \nplume advects northward into the Desert Southwest. Upper level \nforcing will begin to increase ahead of an approaching depending \nupper level trough digging southward across northern CA, and with \nHurricane Polo approaching the southern Baja. Storms will favor \ndevelopment along the Continental Divide before moving east into \nthe Rio Grande Valley and central NM. \n\nMonday sees the upper level trough close off to a 563dm H5 low over \ncentral CA, pulling up Hurricane Polo and its associated abundant \nmoisture midway up the Baja Coast. This will bring numerous to \nwidespread shower and thunderstorm activity across much of NM. Given \nthe increasing upper level shear, some storms are likely to have a \nsevere weather component alongside the risk of flash flooding from \nstorms training over the same areas. This next round of widespread \nshower and thunderstorm activity will likely continue across NM \nnonstop through Monday night and Tuesday as the H5 low tracks across \nAZ, absorbing the remnants of Polo moving ashore northwestern \nMexico. Depending on how the dynamics of this system absorption \noccurs, shower and thunderstorm activity over NM may persist as long \nas Friday. Many areas will likely see another 1 to 3 inches with \nlocalized amounts even higher. This will exacerbate any flash \nflooding impacts leftover from this week's event, produce rises on \narea tributaries, streams, and rivers. If not already prepped from \nthe current ongoing weather, now is the time to double-check your \nemergency plans and be weather aware.",
